{"type":"npc","name":"Pilatatry","folder":null,"system":{"attributes":{"hp":{"value":60,"temp":0,"max":60,"details":""},"ac":{"value":21,"details":""},"allSaves":{"value":""},"speed":{"value":25,"otherSpeeds":[],"details":""},"immunities":[{"type":"poison","exceptions":[]}],"weaknesses":[{"type":"cold-iron","value":4,"exceptions":[]}]},"initiative":{"statistic":"perception"},"details":{"languages":{"value":["common","sakvroth","venenan"],"details":"Speak with animals"},"level":{"value":4},"blurb":"","publicNotes":"<p>Pilatatries are the bane of farming communities and foodies worldwide. They sneak onto farms in the form of small animals and secretly corrupt the livestock for no purpose other than to see people who eat them become sick. It's quite tricky to detect the handiwork of a pilatatry other than a very specific and unusual aftertaste of the cooked food or the sight of an unknown farmhand walking the fields. But even when caught, they can fool most people with quick talking or barter their way out of trouble with trinkets and gossip.</p><p>Pilatatries tend not to stick around a place for too long, just long enough to spread their poison and ensure it takes effect. However, they do linger longer for chances to ruin festivals and fancy banquets. They love nothing more than watching people spit out food that took months to prepare. Pilatatries often keep ledgers with the dates of such feasts and their travel time, ensuring that they can ruin such events year after year.</p><p>Pilatatries' innate poison is quick to expire, so they often lace livestock products with an inert version and provide the necessary magic to activate it only at the key moment. To augment their toxic capabilities, pilatatries also learn to make stable mimicries of a variety of other toxins out of corrupted animal products. Hags are often well aware of any nearby pilatatries and tend to visit them to trade for poisons. Pilatatries are proud that powerful creatures value their services and occasionally grant themselves the title of hag lieutenant. When in this state, they seek out other gremlins to subjugate and present to the hag as new subjects. Most hags ignore the antics of these lower creatures, but a select few use these armies to deal with rivals or spread corruption in new and unusual ways, like luring adventurers into deadly traps.</p><h2>Purveyors of Royal Foodstuffs</h2><p>A pilatatry can be just as status-minded as any other creature, and the most snobbish among them take pride in corrupting the most prestigious events possible. Why sicken a mere farming village when you can ruin a royal banquet? For pilatatries, infiltrating high society is in some ways easier than sneaking into a commoners' festival. After all, everyone in an isolated village knows one another, while amongst nobility, no one pays attention to the hordes of servants underfoot. For a pilatatry at a grand event, the greatest risk is in running into a rival pilatatry out to steal their glory.</p><h2>Suggested Monster Parts</h2><p><strong>Monster Parts</strong> 12/27/50 gp</p><p><strong>Eligible Refinements</strong> weapons (bludgeoning, piercing), armor (light, medium), perception item, skill items (Acrobatics, Crafting, Deception, Nature, Stealth, Thievery)</p><p><strong>Eligible Imbued Properties</strong> bane, dexterity, elongating, energy resistant (vitality), intelligence, poison, poisonous armor, sensory (low-light vision), spells (Primal: @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.spells-srd.6Ot4N22t5tPD51BO]{Knock}, @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.spells-srd.gfPjmG6Fe6D3MFjl]{Pest Form}, @UUID[Compendium.pf2e.spells-srd.BBvV7qoXGdw09q1C]{Speak with Animals}), wild</p>","privateNotes":"","publication":{"title":"Battlezoo Bestiary: Elemental Storm (Pathfinder)","authors":"Lars Hach","license":"ORC","remaster":true}},"resources":{},"_migration":{"version":0.942,"previous":{"schema":0.941,"foundry":"13.347","system":"7.3.1"}},"abilities":{"str":{"mod":2},"dex":{"mod":4},"con":{"mod":2},"int":{"mod":5},"